# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Real situations (problem -> thinking -> action -> reason) and field notes for each Hemodialysis Technician module.
Each scenario: (title, what you see, what is likely going on, what to do, why it matters)."""

SITUATIONS = {
"01": {"scenarios": [
    ("Two patients, one surname", "Two patients named Santos are booked on the same morning shift. At 06:40 a treatment record is already open at station 3 when one of them sits down in the chair.",
     "The open record may belong to the other Santos. Similar names are one of the most common causes of wrong-patient errors, and a busy start makes the mistake easy.",
     "Ask the patient to state full name and date of birth, and compare both with the record. If either one differs, stop the setup and tell the nurse.",
     "A dialyzer, a dialysate, and a fluid goal set up for someone else can harm the person who is actually in the chair."),
    ("&ldquo;Just do it, I&rsquo;ll sign later&rdquo;", "At the start of a crowded afternoon shift, a coworker asks you to start a task you have only watched. She promises to sign your competency form afterward.",
     "The request skips supervision. A signature written after the fact does not make the task safe while you are doing it.",
     "Say plainly that you have not been signed off, and ask your preceptor or the nurse how to proceed. Offer to help with the tasks you are cleared for.",
     "Scope protects the patient, and it protects you. An unassigned task is a reportable event even when nothing goes wrong."),
    ("A phone call asking about a patient", "At 09:15 the unit phone rings. The caller says she is a patient&rsquo;s daughter and asks whether her mother came for dialysis today.",
     "You cannot confirm who is calling, or whether the patient wants information shared with this person.",
     "Do not confirm or deny anything. Take the caller&rsquo;s name and number, and pass the call to the nurse, following the unit&rsquo;s disclosure procedure.",
     "The Data Privacy Act protects health information. Even confirming that a patient came today is a disclosure."),
    ("A wrong entry on the wrong machine", "Halfway through the morning you realize that you recorded a pre-use check under machine 6, when the machine you actually checked was machine 9.",
     "Two records are now wrong. Machine 6 appears checked when it was not, and machine 9 appears unchecked when it was.",
     "Tell the nurse. Correct both entries by the approved method, so that the original entry stays readable, and check machine 6 before anyone uses it.",
     "The next person decides from the record whether a machine is safe. A quiet fix hides a real gap."),
 ], "tips": [
    "At the start of every shift, find out three things: who your supervising nurse is, which machines or stations are on hold, and where the emergency cart is.",
    "Write down exact words and times. &ldquo;07:10, patient said &lsquo;I feel dizzy&rsquo;&rdquo; tells the nurse far more than &ldquo;patient unwell.&rdquo;",
    "When you are unsure whether something is worth reporting, report it. The nurse can decide it was nothing; you cannot make that decision for the nurse.",
    "Never photograph records, screens, or patients with your own phone.",
 ]},
"02": {"scenarios": [
    ("Shoes that no longer fit", "Mr. Danilo Reyes, 58, steps on the scale at 07:05. He is 3.1 kg above his last post-treatment weight and says his slippers feel tight. He is breathing comfortably and talking in full sentences.",
     "Most of the gain is probably fluid. He has no urgent symptom right now, but the gain is larger than usual for him.",
     "Record both weights. Ask about breathlessness and chest discomfort, and report the numbers and his symptoms to the nurse before setup goes on.",
     "The nurse and physician decide how much fluid to remove and how fast. To decide well, they need the exact figures and how he feels."),
    ("A patient who still passes urine", "During a quiet moment, a new technician is surprised to see a patient on dialysis walk to the toilet to urinate.",
     "Many patients keep some kidney function for months or years. That function helps remove fluid and wastes between sessions.",
     "Treat it as normal and useful information. If the patient says the urine has dropped sharply, pass that on to the nurse.",
     "A fall in urine output changes how much fluid builds up between sessions, and it may change the treatment plan."),
    ("A critical result on the fax tray", "At 08:30 a laboratory report marked &ldquo;critical: potassium&rdquo; is lying on the printer tray. The patient is not due until the afternoon shift.",
     "A high potassium can stop the heart without any warning sign. Nobody may have seen the report yet.",
     "Hand the report to the nurse immediately, and say whose it is and when it printed. Do not leave it for later, and do not try to call the patient yourself.",
     "Critical results have their own urgent reporting pathway for a reason: the delay is the danger."),
    ("Tired and pale", "Mrs. Corazon Dela Cruz, 66, tells you while you take her weight that she has been unusually tired for two weeks and gets breathless climbing the stairs to her apartment. Her weight gain is modest.",
     "Tiredness has many causes. Anemia is common in kidney failure, because the kidneys make less of the hormone that drives red blood cell production.",
     "Report what she said, in her own words, and how long it has lasted. Do not suggest a cause or a medicine.",
     "The team checks hemoglobin, iron, and other causes. Your part is to make sure the symptom is heard."),
 ], "tips": [
    "One liter of water weighs about one kilogram. That is why the scale is the simplest fluid monitor in the unit.",
    "Always note whether a weight was taken before or after treatment, and with or without shoes, bags, or a wheelchair.",
    "A patient who &ldquo;feels fine&rdquo; can still have a dangerous laboratory result. Symptoms and numbers are separate pieces of information.",
    "Compare each reading with the patient&rsquo;s own usual pattern. A 2 kg gain may be ordinary for one person and alarming for another.",
 ]},
"03": {"scenarios": [
    ("&ldquo;Can I skip Saturday? I have a wedding.&rdquo;", "On a Wednesday, while you check his lines, a patient asks you quietly whether missing one session really matters.",
     "If the answer sounds discouraging or dismissive, he may simply skip without telling anyone.",
     "Thank him for asking. Say that it does matter, and bring the nurse over so that the session can be rescheduled.",
     "Missed sessions lead to fluid overload and high potassium. Most units can move a session if they know in advance."),
    ("A leaflet that confused the family", "A patient&rsquo;s son arrives with printouts. He read online that peritoneal dialysis is &ldquo;better,&rdquo; and he wants his father switched this week.",
     "The family is motivated, but they are working from general information. Whether a treatment suits his father depends on the patient, the home, and the local program.",
     "Explain the basic difference using the unit&rsquo;s approved material. Write down their questions, and arrange a conversation with the nurse or nephrologist.",
     "Good decisions come from the patient&rsquo;s goals and the team&rsquo;s assessment together, not from which option sounds better."),
    ("No ride home", "At 11:20, as her treatment ends, a patient says her usual tricycle did not come and she has no money for another ride.",
     "Transport problems are a leading reason that patients miss the next session.",
     "Tell the nurse before the patient leaves. Units often have a social worker or a contact list for exactly this.",
     "Solving today&rsquo;s ride protects Thursday&rsquo;s treatment."),
    ("&ldquo;I am tired of this.&rdquo;", "Mr. Rogelio Santos, who has dialyzed in the unit for years, says quietly during his second hour that he is thinking of stopping dialysis.",
     "This may be fatigue, low mood, a practical problem, or a considered wish. It is always serious.",
     "Listen without arguing. Tell him you want the nurse to hear this, and report it the same day in his own words.",
     "The care team can treat symptoms, depression, and burdens, and can discuss conservative care with him properly. Silence helps nobody."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Ask &ldquo;What is the hardest part for you right now?&rdquo; before you explain anything.",
    "Use the unit&rsquo;s approved leaflets. Do not make up your own comparisons between treatments.",
    "After you explain, ask the patient to tell you the plan in their own words. If it comes back wrong, change your explanation, not the patient.",
    "Record what you reinforced and whom you told. Otherwise the next shift will not know.",
 ]},
"04": {"scenarios": [
    ("&ldquo;Take me off twenty minutes early.&rdquo;", "At hour three, a patient asks to finish early because traffic on the highway is bad and he wants to beat the rush.",
     "Every minute taken off the clock is cleaning that does not happen, and it may also leave fluid behind.",
     "Acknowledge his reason and tell the nurse. Do not change the time on the machine yourself.",
     "Three sessions of four hours cover about 12 of the week&rsquo;s 168 hours. Losing 20 minutes regularly adds up to a missed session each month."),
    ("The wrong dialyzer on the hook", "During setup at 06:30 you notice that the dialyzer hanging at the station is a different model from the one on the order.",
     "Someone picked the wrong box, or the order has changed. Dialyzers differ in size and in how much they clean.",
     "Stop setup. Show the nurse the order and the dialyzer, and replace the dialyzer only on the nurse&rsquo;s instruction.",
     "The dialyzer is part of the prescription, like the dose of a medicine."),
    ("Conductivity alarm after a jug change", "A machine alarms for low conductivity one minute after a concentrate container was replaced.",
     "The pick-up tube may be out of the fluid, the container may be the wrong type, or air may have entered the line. The machine has gone into bypass.",
     "Check that the tube is seated and that the label matches the order. If the alarm does not clear promptly, tell the nurse. Do not silence it again and again.",
     "In bypass, the patient is not being dialyzed. A wrong concentrate is a medication error."),
    ("A post-dialysis sample drawn late", "On adequacy day, the blood sample at the end of treatment was collected ten minutes after the blood pump was stopped.",
     "After dialysis stops, urea moves back into the blood from the tissues. A late sample therefore reads higher, and the result looks worse than the treatment was.",
     "Tell the nurse exactly when the sample was drawn, so that the result is interpreted or repeated correctly. Do not relabel the time.",
     "Prescription changes are made on these numbers. A mistimed sample can lead to the wrong change."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Blood and dialysate must flow in opposite directions through the dialyzer. If the dialysate hoses are connected the wrong way around, cleaning drops.",
    "A weight gain and a fluid-removal goal are different numbers. Only the confirmed order goes into the machine.",
    "Read the concentrate label every time, even when the jug &ldquo;is always the same.&rdquo;",
    "When treatment is interrupted, note when it stopped and when it restarted. Delivered time is what counts.",
 ]},
"05": {"scenarios": [
    ("Same gloves, next station", "At 10:00 you see a coworker adjust a machine at station 2 and walk straight to the patient at station 3, still wearing the same gloves.",
     "Gloves carry germs and blood from one patient to the next exactly as bare hands do.",
     "Speak up politely, at once: &ldquo;Gloves.&rdquo; If it keeps happening, tell the nurse.",
     "Most infections in dialysis units travel on hands and gloves. Reminding each other is part of the job, not rudeness."),
    ("Blood on the floor", "After needle removal at the end of the morning shift, there are a few drops of blood on the floor beside a chair.",
     "Blood on the floor is a slip hazard and an infection hazard. Wiping it with a dry tissue only spreads it.",
     "Keep people away and put on gloves. Absorb the blood with disposable material, then clean and disinfect the area by the unit&rsquo;s spill procedure, and discard the waste as infectious.",
     "Hepatitis B virus can survive for days in dried blood on surfaces."),
    ("A needlestick", "While clearing a station between shifts, you feel a prick through your glove from a needle that was left in the linen.",
     "This is an occupational exposure, no matter who left the needle there.",
     "Wash the site with soap and water, tell the nurse immediately, and follow the exposure procedure within the same hour.",
     "Treatment after an exposure works best when it is started within hours. Embarrassment is not a reason to wait."),
    ("The isolation machine is free", "The unit is one machine short for the afternoon shift. Someone suggests borrowing the machine from the hepatitis B isolation room &ldquo;just for today.&rdquo;",
     "That machine is reserved for a reason. Moving it risks spreading the virus to patients who are not immune.",
     "Do not move it. Tell the charge nurse about the shortage.",
     "Dedicated machines, rooms, and staff for patients with hepatitis B are a core infection-control rule in dialysis."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Gloves on does not mean hands clean. Clean your hands before you put gloves on and after you take them off.",
    "Supplies taken to a station stay there or are discarded. Nothing goes back to the clean area.",
    "The contact time is printed on the disinfectant label. If you do not know it, find out before the next turnover.",
    "Sharps go into the container at the point of use, by the person who used them. Never recap a needle, and never carry one across the room.",
 ]},
"06": {"scenarios": [
    ("Self-test failed, shift waiting", "At 05:45, with the first patients already in the waiting area, a machine fails its pressure self-test. A coworker says, &ldquo;Restart it, that one always passes the second time.&rdquo;",
     "A test that fails sometimes is telling you that something is marginal. Passing on a retry does not explain the failure.",
     "Record the message and tag the machine. Report to the nurse and the biomedical contact, and prepare another machine with its own full set of checks.",
     "The self-test checks the alarms that protect the patient. An unreliable alarm is worse than none, because people trust it."),
    ("A wet transducer protector", "Midway through priming, you see that fluid has reached the small filter on one of the pressure lines.",
     "A wet protector blocks the pressure signal, and fluid or blood may have reached the machine side.",
     "Tell the nurse. The protector is replaced by the unit&rsquo;s method, and if fluid passed through it, the machine is checked before use.",
     "False pressure readings give false reassurance, and blood inside the machine is an infection risk for the next patient."),
    ("Air alarm during priming", "The air detector alarms again and again while you prime the circuit for the first patient of the day.",
     "Usually the level in the venous chamber is low, or the line is not seated in the detector. Sometimes the detector is dirty.",
     "Reseat the line and correct the level by the approved priming method. If the alarm continues, hold the machine and report it.",
     "An air detector that alarms wrongly during priming may also fail to alarm when it should."),
    ("The disinfection log is blank", "At the start of the shift a machine shows &ldquo;ready,&rdquo; but the log has no record that last night&rsquo;s disinfection ran or that a residual test was done.",
     "You cannot tell whether the machine was disinfected, or whether chemical remains inside it.",
     "Do not use it. Report to the nurse. By procedure, the machine is rinsed and tested, or disinfected again.",
     "Disinfectant reaching a patient&rsquo;s blood, and a machine that was never disinfected, are both serious harms."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Before you call a circuit ready, trace each tube with your finger from one end to the other.",
    "Write alarm messages down exactly, including any code. Nobody can diagnose &ldquo;It beeped.&rdquo;",
    "Never silence an alarm before you know why it sounded.",
    "The instructions for use differ between models. What was right on the old machine may be wrong on the new one.",
 ]},
"07": {"scenarios": [
    ("Chlorine just detectable", "At 05:30, the morning total chlorine test after the first carbon tank shows a faint color, below the limit. Last week the same test showed none.",
     "The first tank may be nearing exhaustion, or the city may have raised its dose.",
     "Record the number, test after the second tank, and report the change even though the result is within the limit.",
     "Catching the trend lets the carbon be replaced before chlorine reaches a patient."),
    ("Bicarbonate from yesterday", "While preparing for the morning shift, you find a mixed bicarbonate container labeled with yesterday&rsquo;s date.",
     "Mixed bicarbonate supports bacterial growth, and it loses strength over time.",
     "Do not use it. Discard it by the unit&rsquo;s method, tell the nurse, and prepare a fresh batch.",
     "Old bicarbonate risks contaminated dialysate and a wrong mix."),
    ("Two acid formulas, similar jugs", "On the concentrate shelf, two acid concentrates with different potassium levels sit side by side in nearly identical containers.",
     "This arrangement invites someone to connect the wrong one.",
     "Read the full label against the order every time. Tell the nurse that the way they are stored makes a mix-up likely.",
     "Conductivity will look normal with either formula. Only the label tells them apart."),
    ("Reverse osmosis alarm mid-shift", "At 10:40, with patients on dialysis, the water room alarm sounds for high product conductivity.",
     "The membrane may be failing, or the feed water may have changed. The quality of the water is now in doubt.",
     "Tell the charge nurse immediately, and state which stations are running. Hold new starts. Do not reset the alarm and walk away.",
     "The nurse and physician decide what happens to the patients already connected. They can decide only if they know now."),
 ], "tips": [
    "A normal machine display does not prove the water is safe. Water checks and machine checks answer different questions.",
    "Use a total chlorine test, not a free chlorine test. Only total chlorine detects chloramine.",
    "Write the number, not a tick.",
    "If the softener clock is wrong after a power outage, tell the water technician before the shift starts.",
 ]},
"08": {"scenarios": [
    ("The buzz is gone", "Mrs. Teresita Mendoza, 61, arrives on Monday morning and says, &ldquo;My fistula feels quiet since last night.&rdquo;",
     "The access may have clotted. A clot can sometimes be cleared if it is treated quickly.",
     "Do not prepare for needles. Tell the nurse immediately, with the time the patient first noticed the change.",
     "Every hour counts when a clotted access is to be saved."),
    ("A wet catheter dressing", "During the rainy season, a patient arrives soaked from a downpour. The dressing over his chest catheter is damp and lifting at one edge.",
     "A wet dressing lets bacteria reach the exit site.",
     "Do not press it back down. Tell the nurse, who will assess the site and change the dressing with clean technique.",
     "Catheter infections can spread to the bloodstream and the heart."),
    ("Bleeding that will not stop", "Ten minutes after needle removal, a needle site is still oozing through the gauze.",
     "Prolonged bleeding may point to a narrowing in the access, too much blood thinner, or pressure applied in the wrong place.",
     "Keep the patient seated and tell the nurse. Do not send the patient home until the nurse is satisfied.",
     "Repeated long bleeding times are a warning sign that the team investigates."),
    ("Blood pressure on the wrong arm", "As patients settle in, a new aide is about to wrap a blood pressure cuff around a patient&rsquo;s fistula arm.",
     "The pressure of a cuff can clot the access.",
     "Stop the aide, move the cuff to the other arm, and explain why.",
     "One careless cuff can end an access that took months to create."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Ask every patient on arrival: &ldquo;Any change in your access since last time?&rdquo;",
    "Report redness, warmth, swelling, pain, or drainage at an access the same minute you see it, not at handoff.",
    "Keep the access uncovered during treatment. A blanket over a needle site has hidden fatal bleeding.",
    "Never touch a catheter hub, clamp, or cap unless that task has been assigned to you and you are signed off for it.",
 ]},
"09": {"scenarios": [
    ("Venous pressure alarm", "In the second hour, the venous pressure alarm sounds at station 5. The patient looks comfortable and is watching television.",
     "Often the patient has bent the access arm or is lying on the line. Sometimes the needle has moved, or a clot is forming.",
     "Look at the patient, the access, and the line from the needle to the machine. If a kink is obvious and correcting it is one of your assigned tasks, correct it; otherwise call the nurse. Never reset first and look later.",
     "The alarm tells you where to look. The cause may be harmless, or it may be the start of blood loss."),
    ("&ldquo;I feel hot and I am yawning.&rdquo;", "Two hours into treatment, a patient yawns again and again, says she feels warm, and looks pale.",
     "These are common early signs of a falling blood pressure.",
     "Call the nurse now and stay with the patient. Do not change the machine settings.",
     "Acting at the yawning stage prevents a collapse a few minutes later."),
    ("A weight that cannot be right", "At 07:00 the scale shows a patient 6 kg heavier than three days ago.",
     "The patient may be holding a bag, wearing boots, or sitting in a different wheelchair. Or the weight may be real.",
     "Check identity, remove the extras, weigh again as directed, and report both readings.",
     "A wrong weight entered into the record can lead to far too much fluid being removed."),
    ("Yesterday&rsquo;s settings", "On Wednesday morning a coworker suggests copying Monday&rsquo;s treatment settings because &ldquo;her orders never change.&rdquo;",
     "Orders do change, and the saved settings may belong to a different patient.",
     "Verify today&rsquo;s order with the nurse before anything is entered.",
     "Errors from copying old settings forward are a known cause of wrong-treatment events."),
 ], "tips": [
    "On every round, look at the patient first, then the access, then the lines, then the machine, then the record.",
    "Chart at the time you take the reading. Numbers written from memory at the end of the shift are guesses.",
    "A patient who has gone quiet or sleepy needs a closer look, not less attention.",
    "End every handoff with what is still open and who owns it.",
 ]},
"10": {"scenarios": [
    ("A cramp that makes him shout", "In the last hour of treatment, a patient cries out and grabs his calf. The muscle is visibly knotted.",
     "Cramps often mean that fluid is being removed faster than the body can refill the bloodstream.",
     "Call the nurse and stay with the patient. Report when the cramp started and whether he has any dizziness or nausea.",
     "The nurse decides whether to change the treatment. A cramp can come just before a fall in blood pressure."),
    ("Blood under the blanket", "On your round you notice a dark stain spreading across a patient&rsquo;s blanket. The machine is not alarming.",
     "A needle may have slipped out. The machine can keep pumping blood onto the bed without an alarm.",
     "Shout for help immediately and tell the nurse what you see. Follow the nurse&rsquo;s instructions exactly.",
     "A patient can lose a dangerous amount of blood in a few minutes."),
    ("Three patients with chills", "Within twenty minutes, three patients on the same side of the unit complain of chills and ask for extra blankets.",
     "When several patients become unwell together, think of something they share: the water, the dialysate, or a batch of supplies.",
     "Tell the charge nurse at once and say, &ldquo;Three patients, same time.&rdquo; Tell the water technician.",
     "One patient with chills is a patient problem. Three patients with chills is a unit problem."),
    ("The lights go out", "At 14:20, the power fails in the middle of the shift. The machines switch to battery and begin to alarm.",
     "Battery backup is short and may not run everything. The generator may or may not start.",
     "Stay at your station, reassure the patients, and follow the charge nurse&rsquo;s instructions. Do not start new treatments.",
     "The unit has a plan for this. The plan works only if everyone follows one voice."),
    ("He will not wake up", "A patient who was dozing does not respond when you say his name or touch his shoulder.",
     "This may be sleep, low blood pressure, low blood sugar, or cardiac arrest.",
     "Call for help immediately. If you are trained, begin the basic life support steps you are certified for while help arrives.",
     "Seconds matter. Calling first is never the wrong decision."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Before each shift, know the emergency call method and where the cart and the defibrillator are.",
    "Keep your basic life support certification current.",
    "In an emergency, do the task you are given and say when it is done.",
    "Afterward, write down the times while you still remember them.",
 ]},
"11": {"scenarios": [
    ("A smudged label", "At 06:15 a reprocessed dialyzer passes its checks, but the patient&rsquo;s name on the label is partly rubbed off.",
     "You cannot prove whose dialyzer it is.",
     "Hold it, keep it apart from the others, and report it. Use a new dialyzer if the nurse directs.",
     "Giving a patient another person&rsquo;s dialyzer exposes that patient to the other person&rsquo;s blood."),
    ("Residual test still positive", "After the rinse, the germicide test strip still shows a trace.",
     "Germicide remains in the dialyzer or in the lines.",
     "Do not connect it. Rinse again by procedure and retest. If it stays positive, reject it and report it.",
     "Germicide entering the blood causes burning at the access, low blood pressure, and worse."),
    ("Volume test below the limit", "The total cell volume test shows a dialyzer below the program&rsquo;s acceptance limit.",
     "Too many fibers are clotted for it to clean properly.",
     "Reject it by procedure and record the reason.",
     "A dialyzer that looks fine can deliver much less cleaning than was prescribed."),
    ("Back from repair, no paperwork", "A machine comes back from the workshop and is parked at a station. There is no release form with it.",
     "Nobody on the floor knows what was done to it, or whether the post-repair tests passed.",
     "Do not use it. Ask the nurse to confirm its release with the biomedical technician.",
     "A machine returns to service by a documented decision, not by being wheeled back."),
 ], "tips": [
    "If you cannot prove it, do not use it.",
    "Where the program requires it, two people check the dialyzer against the patient before use. Never sign for a check you did not see.",
    "Germicides irritate the eyes and lungs. Wear the protection the procedure states.",
    "Report repeated faults on the same machine. One event is a repair; three is a pattern.",
 ]},
"12": {"scenarios": [
    ("Everything at once", "At 05:50 you have a patient at the door, a machine showing a failed test, and a water log that is not yet signed.",
     "All three are yours to notice, but not all three are yours to solve.",
     "Deal with safety first: tag the machine, tell the nurse about the machine and the unsigned water log, then verify the patient&rsquo;s identity.",
     "Ordering tasks by risk, not by who is loudest, is the core skill of this job."),
    ("Two instructions that disagree", "The nurse tells you to prepare station 4. A doctor passing by tells you to use station 7 instead.",
     "One of them may not know what the other said.",
     "Tell each of them what the other said, and ask them to agree on one. Do not choose for them.",
     "Conflicting instructions that a technician settles quietly are a classic source of errors."),
    ("A near miss nobody else saw", "Before the first patient was connected, you noticed that you had connected the wrong concentrate, and you fixed it.",
     "No harm occurred, and nobody would know if you said nothing.",
     "Report it through the unit&rsquo;s near-miss process.",
     "The next person may not notice in time. Near-miss reports are how a unit fixes the cause, such as look-alike containers."),
    ("Handoff in a hurry", "At the change from the morning to the afternoon shift, the outgoing technician says, &ldquo;All good, see you tomorrow,&rdquo; and heads for the door.",
     "You do not know what is on hold or what is still pending.",
     "Before the technician leaves, ask three questions: anything on hold, anything unfinished, and anything I should watch.",
     "Most information is lost at shift change."),
 ], "tips": [
    "Verify, pause, report, confirm, record. When in doubt, go back to the first word.",
    "A hold stays in place until the designated person releases it, however long that takes.",
    "Being signed off for a skill on one machine model does not sign you off on another.",
    "Update your logbook every day. It is your evidence of training.",
 ]},
}
